/* ---- Phase 32B: The Golden Mean, developed — Aristotle's "A Scroll of the Nicomachean Ethics" ----
   Idea check: playing this, you feel steadiness grow with practice, and you find that the right middle isn't always the exact center.
   That is Aristotle's idea that virtue is a habit of choosing the mean relative to us.
   Stages: I · Courage (a wolf lunges out of the dark on the left: fear, too little; a fire-dare spirit flares on the right: rashness, too much) →
   II · Generosity (a miser's chest tugs your coins back on the left; a spendthrift flings them into the sea on the right; and the gold band now sits
   a little off-center, a different side each play, and is NOT drawn: the beam hums and glows under you when you're in it) →
   III · Truthfulness (a timid mouse on the left, a strutting peacock on the right; the band starts narrow, but the Habit you've built carries over,
   so it widens quickly). The crossing is split in thirds, one per stage; the far cliff is the win.
   Movement: a lateral "needle" (s.n, -1..1 across the beam's walkable width; s.vn its speed) that the pushes shove and your hold counters.
   You move forward only while the needle is inside the gold band [s.bc-s.bw, s.bc+s.bw]; outside it you pause. Progress never goes backward and you
   never fall (the needle is clamped to the beam). Time in the gold builds Habit (s.h): the band widens faster and the pushes weaken.
   Controls: hold left or right (the pad, the arrow keys, or press and hold either half of the screen). */
